===Etymology 1===
From {{der|en|enm|highten}}, variant of {{m|enm|hoten||to name, to be named}}, from {{der|en|ang|hātan}}. The stem of the word was remodelled by analogy with the simple past form {{m|enm|hight}}, from {{der|en|ang|hēht}}. Cognate with {{m+|sco|hecht}}, {{m+|nl|heten}}, {{m+|de|heißen}}, etc.

====Verb====
{{en-verb|hights|highting|hight|hight}}

# {{lb|en|archaic|transitive}} To [[call]], [[name]].
#: {{ux|en|King Arthur's court was '''hight''' Camelot.}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1590|author=Edmund Spenser|title=The Faerie Queene|chapter=Book II, Canto II|url=https://archive.org/details/bim_early-english-books-1475-1640_the-faerie-queene-dispo_spenser-edmund_1590/page/209|page=209|text=He that made loue vnto the eldeſt Dame, Was '''hight''' Sir ''Huddibras'', an hardy man{{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Byron Childe Harold|canto=I|stanza=III|passage=Childe Harold was he '''hight'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Pyle Round Table|page=66|chapter=The Story of Launcelot|passage=“Lord,” said Sir Launcelot, “I am '''hight''' Launcelot, and am surnamed ‘He of the Lake.’”}}
# {{lb|en|archaic|copulative|with a name as complement}} To be called or named.
#: {{ux|en|I '''hight''' Sir Galahad.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=c. 1547|author=w:Henry Howard, Earl of Surrey|title=The faithful Lover declareth his Pains and his uncertain Joys, and with only Hope recomforteth somewhat his woful Heart|text=Bright was her hue, and Geraldine she '''hight'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Love's Labour's Lost Q1|act=I|scene=i|page=8|passage=There did I ſee that low ſpirited Swaine {{...}} which as I remember, '''hight''' ''Coſtard'' {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|archaic|dialectal}} To [[command]]; to [[enjoin]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1872|author=w:John Stuart Blackie|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=9nACAAAAQAAJ&printsec=frontcover&dq=Lays+of+the+Highlands+and+Islands&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwjZvtHBhtPWAhVPFMAKHf4ACQMQ6AEIKDAA#v=snippet&q=%20I%20obey.&f=false|title=Lays of the Highlands and Islands|text=Malaise priest of Innishmurry / '''Hights ''' me go, and I obey.}}

=====Usage notes=====
*The word survives only in literary and dialectal use. It chiefly occurs in the past tense.

